# OpenSpec Workflow

You are working inside an OpenSpec change. This rule is intentionally thin — it does **not** restate how
to author each artifact. The authoring contract lives in two places, pulled on demand:

- the **`/opsx:*` command** you run (`commands/opsx-*.md`) — source of truth for the phase steps, gates,
  preflight, external-system publish (offer → preview → execute), naming, and directory layout; and
- the **artifact schema** (`openspec/schemas/<schema>/schema.yaml` per-artifact `instruction` +
  `templates/*.md` + `config.yaml` rules), compiled into the OpenSpec instructions when the command runs
  — source of truth for how to generate and validate each artifact (delta discipline, capability
  decomposition, baseline/delta timelessness, reverse-engineered-baseline provenance, spec conventions,
  trace metadata, self-checks).

Do not run a phase from memory: consult the command and the compiled schema instruction for the artifact
you are on. The `openspec-workflow` routing skill maps intent → command. What remains below is only the
cross-cutting orchestration that no single command or artifact owns.

## When to Use (change sizing)

Before running `/opsx:propose`, decide the change size. OpenSpec is not "one size fits all" — applying
the full pipeline to a small change creates bureaucratic artifacts (empty architecture sections, forced
grooming rounds, over-scoped task lists) and wastes hours.

| Category | Criteria | Workflow |
|---|---|---|
| **Quick fix** | < 4h, single file/module, no requirements ambiguity, no external access | Commit directly — skip OpenSpec entirely |
| **Small change** | < 1 day, single package, clear requirements, no grooming needed | `/opsx:propose --schema bugfix` (lean pipeline), or a short-form `feature` with a light — not skipped — design/grooming |
| **Standard feature/fix** | Multi-day, clear scope, some requirements ambiguity | `/opsx:propose --schema feature` or `--schema bugfix` |
| **Cross-team / complex** | Multi-package, architectural impact, multiple stakeholders | Full `feature` pipeline + grooming session |

**Default bias:** start one category smaller than instinct suggests. Escalate only when the lighter
workflow fails to capture something material.

## Update-before-approval gate (MANDATORY — the step that gets skipped)

Any edit to an artifact's body can silently invalidate its projected blocks, so **before you present an
artifact for approval and before you record an Approve, you MUST first run that artifact's update
procedure and re-set its gate boxes.** This holds even when the human just says "approve", and even
mid-session when no `/opsx:*` command is re-invoked (the glob loaded this rule, not the command). Never
chain from an edit straight into approval, and never chain into the next artifact. Approving an artifact
whose blocks were not rebuilt from the CHANGED body is a defect.

What "update" means per artifact — run it FIRST, then stop for approval (the *how* lives in the gate
reference; read it at the gate, not from memory):

| Artifact | Run before approval |
|---|---|
| discovery, design, proposal | Rebuild the derived blocks from the CHANGED body — reader map (`## Executive Summary` / `## Solution Overview`) + `## Document Consumption Guide`, plus the Integration Specification `### Overview` for an integration design — then re-set the gate boxes. → [derived-block-rederivation](mdc:.cursor/rules/workflow/openspec-workflow/references/derived-block-rederivation.md) |
| fsd, isd | Run the FSD/ISD upstream actualization (design / proposal / discovery / specs ← FSD/ISD) before the doc leaves the gate. → [fsd-isd-upstream-actualization](mdc:.cursor/rules/workflow/openspec-workflow/references/fsd-isd-upstream-actualization.md) |
| grooming, specs, technical-design, tasks | No derived blocks — stop, summarize, and record the approval marker only. |

Then the generic gate applies to EVERY artifact: STOP and summarize, offer Approve / Request changes
(plus Walk the questions only when open questions remain), and record a NAMED-HUMAN approval — never
self-approve. The full procedure — gate steps, per-artifact approval markers, and the routed
re-derivation / actualization — lives in one reference you read when you reach a gate, not from memory:

**[artifact-review-gate](mdc:.cursor/rules/workflow/openspec-workflow/references/artifact-review-gate.md)**

### Approval is not uniform — know the marker per artifact

Approval is NOT the same shape for every artifact, and some artifacts carry NO in-file approval at all.
Never invent a box an artifact does not have, and never self-approve — the approver is always a named
human. The gate reference carries the exact wording; this is the WHAT so you stop at the right marker:

| Artifact | Where approval lives |
|---|---|
| discovery, design, proposal, technical-design | in-file `## Review & Approval` — `Approved` box checked + real `Approved by` |
| grooming | in-file `## Grooming Approval Gate` — all boxes checked, every approver a named human |
| tasks | in-file `## Review & Approval` — `Approved for implementation: yes` + real `Approved by` (no checkbox, by parser design) |
| specs, fsd, isd | NO in-file box (specs merge into the timeless baseline; fsd/isd publish to Confluence, where a box would leak) — approval is recorded at the gate's STOP, not in the file |
| stories.md | none — a deterministic script projection, not an authored artifact; it is never separately approved |

An integration design additionally re-derives its Integration Specification `### Overview` (a local
reader-map) alongside the Solution Overview and Document Consumption Guide before it is presented for
approval — see the gate reference.

### FSD/ISD upstream actualization (design / proposal / discovery / specs ← FSD/ISD)

One flow runs UP the pipeline, not down: the FSD/ISD are the client-signed WHAT (the primary downstream
source of truth), so when a team tailors or hardens the contract ON the FSD/ISD, the upstream artifacts can
go stale. At the FSD/ISD review gate — and again at `/opsx:propose-submit` before the artifacts are
committed/published — propagate those changes back into design / proposal / discovery / specs (each within
its ownership) so every upstream stays true to the signed contract. This pass is deliberately OUTSIDE the
eval contour. The full procedure lives in its own reference:

**[fsd-isd-upstream-actualization](mdc:.cursor/rules/workflow/openspec-workflow/references/fsd-isd-upstream-actualization.md)**
